    Section(
      title: 'History & Systems Review:',
      content:
          '- **Cardiac History**: Non-ischemic dilated cardiomyopathy diagnosed age 38, etiology unclear (negative genetic testing, negative viral serologies). Progressive heart failure despite optimal medical therapy. LVEF declined from 25% to 12% over 2 years. HeartMate 3 LVAD implanted 14 months ago as bridge to transplant. Orthotopic heart transplant 3 weeks ago (bicaval technique).\n- **Post-Transplant Course**: Uncomplicated surgery. Extubated POD 1. Acute cellular rejection Grade 2R on POD 10 surveillance biopsy — treated with IV methylprednisolone pulse x 3 days, repeat biopsy POD 17 showed resolution (Grade 0R). Current LVEF 55% on echocardiogram.\n- **Functional Status Pre-Illness**: Elite recreational cyclist (century rides, 5000+ miles/year). Worked as a software engineer (desk job). Independent in all activities.\n- **Current Functional Status**: FIM score 78/126. Requires moderate assist for transfers, supervision for bed mobility. Ambulates 30 feet with rolling walker and contact guard assist before needing to rest (dyspnea and leg fatigue). Cannot climb stairs. ADLs require setup assistance.\n- **Medications**: Tacrolimus 3 mg BID (target trough 10-15 ng/mL), mycophenolate mofetil 1000 mg BID, prednisone 20 mg daily (taper planned), valganciclovir 900 mg daily (CMV prophylaxis), trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole DS 3x/week (PCP prophylaxis), pantoprazole 40 mg daily, magnesium oxide 400 mg BID (tacrolimus-induced hypomagnesemia), insulin glargine 15 units nightly (steroid-induced hyperglycemia)\n- **Psychosocial**: Married, 2 children ages 10 and 13. Wife works part-time as a teacher. He has been unable to work for 18 months. Describes cycling as \'my identity\' and is anxious about losing it permanently.\n- **Review of Systems**: Fatigue, proximal muscle weakness, mild tremor (tacrolimus), frequent loose stools (mycophenolate), hyperglycemia, difficulty sleeping in hospital',
