    Section(
      title: '+ Explain the physiology of the denervated transplanted heart and its implications for exercise prescription. How does the heart rate response differ from a normally innervated heart?',
      content:
          '**Denervated Heart Physiology**:\n\nDuring orthotopic heart transplant, all autonomic nerve connections (sympathetic and parasympathetic) to the donor heart are severed. This creates a **denervated heart** with fundamentally altered exercise physiology:\n\n**1. Resting Heart Rate**:\n- Normal heart: ~60-70 bpm (vagal tone keeps rate low)\n- Denervated heart: **90-110 bpm** (loss of vagal restraint; intrinsic SA node rate)\n- This patient\'s resting HR of 98 is typical and NOT tachycardia requiring treatment\n\n**2. Heart Rate Response to Exercise**:\n- Normal heart: Rapid HR increase via sympathetic activation within seconds of exercise onset\n- Denervated heart: **Delayed, blunted HR response**. HR increases slowly (over 3-6 minutes) via **circulating catecholamines** (epinephrine/norepinephrine from adrenal medulla), NOT direct neural stimulation. Peak HR is typically **20-25% lower** than age-predicted maximum.\n- Denervated heart peak HR: ~130-150 bpm (versus predicted max of ~175 bpm for age 45)\n\n**3. Heart Rate Recovery**:\n- Normal heart: Rapid HR decline after exercise (vagal reactivation)\n- Denervated heart: **Prolonged HR recovery** — HR remains elevated for 5-10+ minutes after exercise cessation because catecholamine clearance is slow. This requires **extended cool-down periods**.\n\n**4. Cardiac Output Augmentation**:\n- In the denervated heart, cardiac output increases primarily through the **Frank-Starling mechanism** (increased venous return leads to increased stroke volume via increased preload) in the early phase of exercise, with catecholamine-mediated contractility and HR increase providing additional augmentation later.\n- This is why a **prolonged warm-up** is essential — the heart needs time to transition.\n\n**5. Response to Medications**:\n- Denervated hearts do NOT respond to **atropine** (no vagal nerve to block)\n- DO respond to **direct-acting agents**: isoproterenol, epinephrine, dobutamine (act directly on cardiac receptors)\n- Do NOT respond to **indirect-acting agents**: digoxin (vagal-mediated effects are absent)\n\n**6. Reinnervation**:\n- Partial sympathetic reinnervation may occur over **1-5 years** in some patients\n- Evidence: improved HR response, HR variability, and anginal sensation\n- Typically incomplete; exercise prescriptions should assume denervation for at least the first 1-2 years\n\n**Implications for Exercise Prescription**:\n- **Extended warm-up** (10-15 minutes minimum) to allow catecholamine-mediated HR response\n- **Extended cool-down** (10-15 minutes minimum) to allow catecholamine clearance\n- **RPE-based intensity monitoring** (Borg scale) rather than target heart rate, since HR response is unreliable\n- **Avoid sudden high-intensity activity** — the heart cannot rapidly increase output\n- **No isometric Valsalva maneuvers** early post-transplant (sternal precautions + preload sensitivity)',
    ),
    Section(
      title: '+ What are the signs of allograft rejection, and how does immunosuppression affect the rehabilitation plan?',
      content:
          '**Allograft Rejection — Recognition in Rehabilitation**:\n\n**Types of Rejection**:\n1. **Hyperacute**: Occurs within minutes to hours. Pre-formed antibodies. Managed intraoperatively. Not a rehabilitation concern.\n2. **Acute Cellular Rejection (ACR)**: Most common. T-cell mediated. Highest risk in first 3-6 months. Graded 0R to 3R on endomyocardial biopsy. This patient had Grade 2R (moderate) — now resolved.\n3. **Antibody-Mediated Rejection (AMR)**: B-cell/antibody mediated. Can be acute or chronic. Detected by immunohistochemistry.\n4. **Chronic Allograft Vasculopathy (CAV)**: Progressive coronary artery disease in the transplanted heart. Leading cause of late graft failure. Develops over years.\n\n**Signs of Rejection That Rehabilitation Staff Must Recognize**:\n- **New or worsening fatigue** out of proportion to activity level\n- **Declining exercise tolerance** — failure to progress or regression in therapy\n- **New arrhythmias** — atrial fibrillation, ventricular ectopy (denervated hearts are prone to atrial arrhythmias with rejection)\n- **Unexplained weight gain** (fluid retention)\n- **New peripheral edema, elevated JVP, or S3 gallop**\n- **Low-grade fever** without other source\n- **Hypotension** or signs of low cardiac output\n- **Important**: Because the heart is denervated, patients will **NOT experience angina** even with significant rejection or CAV. Ischemia is "silent."\n\n**Action**: Any of these signs should prompt immediate notification of the transplant team and consideration of holding therapy pending evaluation. Endomyocardial biopsy is the gold standard for diagnosis.\n\n**Immunosuppression Implications for Rehabilitation**:\n\n1. **Infection Risk** (highest in first 3-6 months):\n- **Gym hygiene**: Wipe all equipment before and after use. Avoid shared mats. Hand hygiene strictly enforced.\n- **Avoid group exercise classes** with many participants in the early post-transplant period.\n- **Avoid gardening or soil contact** (Aspergillus, Nocardia risk).\n- **Monitor for fever**: Low threshold for infectious workup. Temperature >38C should prompt notification of transplant team.\n\n2. **Steroid Myopathy** (prednisone):\n- Proximal muscle weakness is exacerbated by corticosteroids. This patient\'s proximal weakness (hip flexors 3+/5) is likely multifactorial — deconditioning + steroid myopathy.\n- As prednisone is tapered, strength should improve. Resistance training accelerates recovery.\n\n3. **Osteoporosis Risk**:\n- Corticosteroids + tacrolimus both increase bone loss. DEXA scan recommended at 6-12 months post-transplant.\n- Weight-bearing exercise is protective. Calcium and vitamin D supplementation.\n\n4. **Tremor** (tacrolimus):\n- Fine tremor may affect hand function and balance. Monitor during therapy.\n\n5. **Renal Function**:\n- Tacrolimus is nephrotoxic. Monitor creatinine. Ensure adequate hydration during exercise.\n- Avoid NSAIDs (further nephrotoxicity).\n\n6. **Diabetes/Hyperglycemia**:\n- Tacrolimus and prednisone both cause hyperglycemia. Monitor glucose before and after exercise. Exercise will improve insulin sensitivity.',
    ),
