    Section(
      title: '+ Outline a phased exercise rehabilitation program for this heart transplant recipient, including inpatient and outpatient components.',
      content:
          '**Phase I — Inpatient Rehabilitation (Current, Weeks 3-6 Post-Transplant)**:\n\n*Aerobic Training*:\n- **Warm-up**: 10-15 minutes of slow walking or seated cycling at very low resistance. Essential for catecholamine-mediated HR increase.\n- **Exercise**: Walk or cycle 10-20 minutes at RPE 11-13 (Borg 6-20 scale = "fairly light" to "somewhat hard"). Start with interval format: 3-5 minutes exercise / 2 minutes rest. Progress to continuous exercise.\n- **Cool-down**: 10-15 minutes of slow walking tapering to seated rest. Allow HR to return toward resting level (prolonged recovery expected).\n- **Frequency**: 2 sessions daily (AM and PM), 5-6 days/week.\n- **Monitoring**: HR, BP, RPE, SpO2 before, during, and after exercise. Telemetry monitoring recommended for first 2 weeks of rehabilitation.\n\n*Resistance Training*:\n- **Sternal precautions**: No lifting >5 lbs with upper extremities, no pushing/pulling, no bilateral arm elevation above shoulders for 6-8 weeks post-sternotomy.\n- **Lower extremity focus initially**: Seated knee extensions, hip flexion, ankle pumps, standing hip abduction with support. 1-2 sets of 10-15 reps at low resistance.\n- **After sternal precautions lifted (6-8 weeks)**: Add upper extremity resistance training starting with resistance bands, progressing to light weights.\n\n*Functional Training*:\n- Progressive transfers: bed to chair, chair to standing, sit-to-stand repetitions\n- Gait training: advance from rolling walker to single-point cane to independent\n- Stair training: start with 4-6 steps, progress to full flight\n- ADL training with OT: bathing, dressing, meal preparation\n\n*Discharge Criteria*: Independent transfers, ambulating 500+ feet independently, climbing one flight of stairs with rail, independent ADLs, FIM score >100.\n\n**Phase II — Outpatient Cardiac Rehabilitation (Weeks 6-18 Post-Transplant)**:\n\n*Structure*: Formal cardiac rehab program 3 days/week, ECG-monitored sessions.\n- **Aerobic**: Progress to 30-45 minutes continuous exercise. Modalities: treadmill, cycling, elliptical. RPE-guided intensity (RPE 12-14, "somewhat hard").\n- **Resistance**: After sternal clearance, progressive resistance training 2-3 days/week. All major muscle groups. 2-3 sets of 10-15 reps. Progress load by 5% when completing 15 reps easily.\n- **Flexibility**: Daily stretching, especially chest/shoulder to counteract sternal healing posture.\n\n**Phase III — Community/Independent Exercise (Months 4-12+)**:\n\n- Transition to independent exercise with ongoing physiatrist or cardiologist oversight.\n- **Aerobic goal**: 150-300 minutes/week moderate intensity. Cycling can be reintroduced.\n- **Resistance goal**: 2-3 sessions/week, progressive loading targeting functional goals.\n- **Reassessment**: Cardiopulmonary exercise testing (CPET) at 6 and 12 months to objectively measure VO2 peak and guide exercise intensity.\n\n**Regarding Competitive Cycling**:\n- Transplant recipients CAN return to recreational cycling and even organized events (Transplant Games of America include cycling).\n- **Timeline**: Typically 6-12 months post-transplant before return to vigorous sustained activity.\n- **Limitations**: VO2 peak in transplant recipients is typically **60-70% of age-predicted normal** even with optimal training, due to chronotropic incompetence and peripheral factors.\n- **Century rides (100 miles)**: Possible but will require adjusted expectations for pace and completion time. Hydration and thermoregulation are critical (denervated heart has altered thermoregulatory responses).\n- **Competition**: Encourage Transplant Games rather than open competition — more appropriate peer group and safety infrastructure.',
