    Section(
      title: '+ Describe transplant team coordination, lifelong follow-up requirements, and return-to-work planning for heart transplant recipients.',
      content:
          '**Lifelong Transplant Follow-Up**:\n\n1. **Endomyocardial Biopsy Schedule**: Weekly for month 1, biweekly months 2-3, monthly months 3-6, every 2-3 months for year 1, then annually or as indicated. This is the gold standard for rejection surveillance.\n2. **Cardiac Catheterization/Angiography**: Annual to screen for cardiac allograft vasculopathy (CAV). May include intravascular ultrasound (IVUS) for early detection.\n3. **Echocardiography**: Serial echocardiograms to monitor graft function (LVEF, diastolic function, valve competence).\n4. **Laboratory Monitoring**: Tacrolimus trough levels (weekly initially, then monthly), renal function (BUN/Cr, GFR), CBC, hepatic panel, lipid panel, glucose/HbA1c, CMV/EBV viral loads.\n5. **Cancer Screening**: Immunosuppressed patients have 2-4x increased cancer risk. Annual dermatology skin exams (skin cancer), age-appropriate cancer screenings, vigilance for post-transplant lymphoproliferative disorder (PTLD).\n6. **Bone Health**: DEXA scan at 1 year, then every 2 years. Osteoporosis prevention and treatment.\n7. **Vaccination**: Annual influenza (inactivated only — no live vaccines). Pneumococcal, hepatitis B. No live vaccines (no MMR, varicella, live influenza, or shingles live vaccine; Shingrix recombinant is acceptable).\n\n**Medication Adherence — Critical Issue**:\n- **Non-adherence to immunosuppression is the leading preventable cause of graft loss**.\n- Discuss medication management at every rehabilitation visit.\n- Pill organizers, phone alarms, pharmacy auto-refill, family involvement.\n- Address cost barriers — transplant medications can cost \$2,000-3,000/month. Social work referral for copay assistance programs (manufacturer programs, NeedyMeds, Patient Access Network Foundation).\n\n**Transplant Team Coordination**:\n- **Transplant cardiologist**: Primary coordinator of medical management, rejection surveillance, immunosuppression adjustment.\n- **Physiatrist/Cardiac Rehab**: Exercise prescription, functional recovery, return to activity guidance.\n- **Transplant coordinator**: Scheduling biopsies, labs, imaging. Patient\'s primary point of contact.\n- **Social worker**: Insurance navigation, financial counseling, disability applications, return-to-work coordination.\n- **Psychologist**: Adjustment to transplant, body image (scar, weight changes), guilt about donor, anxiety about rejection.\n- **Dietitian**: Weight management, diabetic diet, renal diet if needed.\n\n**Return-to-Work Planning**:\n- **Timeline**: Most transplant recipients who were working before illness can return to work within 3-6 months post-transplant. This patient\'s desk job (software engineering) has low physical demands.\n- **Considerations**: Frequent medical appointments (biopsies, labs) require flexible scheduling. Infection risk in first 6 months — discuss workplace accommodations (private office vs open plan, hand hygiene access).\n- **Disability**: He has been unable to work for 18 months (LVAD period + transplant). If on SSDI, Ticket to Work program allows trial work period without losing benefits.\n- **ADA protections**: Transplant recipients are protected under the Americans with Disabilities Act. Reasonable accommodations include flexible scheduling for medical appointments and modified work environment during high immunosuppression periods.',
    ),
    Section(
      title: 'DOMAIN E: INTERPERSONAL AND COMMUNICATION SKILLS',
      content:
          'During the initial rehabilitation evaluation, the patient says: "Before I got sick, I was averaging 200 miles a week on my bike. I did the Death Ride twice. Cycling is who I am. I need you to tell me straight — will I ever race again, or is that life over?"',
    ),
    Section(
      title: '+ How would you manage this patient\'s expectations about returning to competitive cycling while maintaining his motivation for rehabilitation?',
      content:
          '**Model Response**:\n\n**1. Acknowledge how central cycling is to his identity**:\n\'I can see how important cycling is to you — it is not just a hobby, it is part of who you are. Completing the Death Ride twice puts you in an elite category of endurance athletes. I respect that, and I want to work with you, not against that identity.\'\n\n**2. Give an honest, direct answer — he asked for straight talk**:\n\'You asked me to be straight with you, so I will be. The physiology of a transplanted heart is different from what you had before. Your new heart does not respond to your nervous system the way your original heart did. It relies on circulating hormones to speed up, which means slower acceleration, a lower peak heart rate, and a longer recovery. Most transplant recipients achieve about 60 to 70 percent of their predicted maximum exercise capacity, even with excellent training.\'\n\n**3. Reframe what is possible rather than dwelling on limitations**:\n\'Here is the good news. Heart transplant recipients absolutely ride bikes — some ride long distances. There are transplant recipients who have completed centuries, triathlons, and even multi-day cycling tours. The Transplant Games of America have competitive cycling events. You will ride again. But your racing will likely look different — you may not match your pre-illness speeds or power output, and your warm-up needs to be longer because of how the transplanted heart responds to exercise.\'\n\n**4. Create a progressive goal framework**:\n\'Rather than focusing on century rides right now, let us build a ladder of goals:\n- **Month 1-2**: Walk independently, climb stairs, begin stationary cycling in cardiac rehab.\n- **Month 3-4**: Ride a stationary bike for 30-45 minutes continuously. Begin outdoor cycling on flat, safe terrain.\n- **Month 6**: Complete a 20-30 mile ride at your own pace.\n- **Month 9-12**: Attempt a 50-mile ride. Consider entering a Transplant Games cycling event.\n- **Year 2+**: Evaluate century ride feasibility based on CPET results and training progression.\nEach goal builds on the last. We will celebrate every milestone.\'\n\n**5. Address the deeper fear — loss of identity**:\n\'I think what you are really asking is whether you will still be you after this transplant. And the answer is yes. You are still a cyclist. You are still someone who takes on the Death Ride. The numbers on the speedometer may change, but the drive and discipline that got you to those starting lines is still there. That mental toughness is exactly what will make you successful in rehabilitation.\'\n\n**6. Provide a role model**:\n\'I would like to connect you with another transplant recipient who is an endurance athlete. Hearing from someone who has been where you are and is back on the bike can be more motivating than anything I can say. Would you be open to that?\'\n\n**Key principles demonstrated**: Honest communication as requested, respecting the patient\'s identity, realistic goal-setting with progressive milestones, reframing limitations as adaptations rather than losses, offering peer mentorship, maintaining motivation while managing expectations.',
    ),
